142	\section{Problem setting and design principle}
143	
144	A case specifies the buyer's category goal, a set of mandatory requirements, the negotiable
145	fields with their admissible options, and one or more suppliers, each with an opening quotation.
146	The score of a case is $(C_\mathrm{open}-C_\mathrm{deal})/(C_\mathrm{open}-C_\mathrm{best})$.
147	The arena imposes a round limit per thread (ten buyer messages by default) and allows 60 seconds per reply, and the final round is
148	conducted without human intervention.
149	
150	Language models interpret a counterpart's correspondence well, but their arithmetic cannot be
151	relied upon without verification. We therefore assign \emph{every binding quantity to code}:
152	prices, term sheets, acceptance and withdrawal are computed and verified with the arena's
153	published cost function and its check of the mandatory requirements. Models either select from
154	enumerated options or produce text that is subsequently filtered. Each model invocation is
155	subject to a timeout with a deterministic fallback, so that a move is issued within the reply deadline even when a model fails. Decisions are conditioned on the evidence available about a supplier and never on a
156	case or supplier identifier.
157	
158	\section{Preparation}
159	
160	Before a session is opened, and hence before any reply deadline applies, a research team is
161	allotted 40 seconds per case (Figure~\ref{fig:system}, top).
162	
163	\paragraph{Specialists} Four subagents each examine one category of data and report at most four
164	findings together with an assessment of which party holds the leverage. \emph{Economics}
165	considers our should-cost estimate, the supplier's price history and the indices of its cost
166	drivers. \emph{Persona} considers the company profile, correspondence, meeting notes and news
167	coverage. \emph{History} considers previous negotiations and the contract with its clauses.
168	\emph{Leverage} considers delivery and quality performance, our demand forecast, our purchase
169	orders and competing quotations.
170	
171	\paragraph{Lead researcher} The lead consolidates these findings, together with the outcomes of
172	comparable earlier cases, into a strategy comprising at most two term trades, the ordering of our
173	arguments and the tone of the correspondence. It determines neither prices nor the pace of
174	concession.
175	
176	\paragraph{Rehearsal} A supplier subagent, acting in character, states the arguments it intends
177	to advance and the weaknesses it hopes will go unnoticed. A buyer subagent then responds to each
178	argument. The outcome is a playbook of anticipated arguments paired with prepared responses.
179	
180	\paragraph{Validation} A proposed trade is admitted only if it is an admissible option of a
181	negotiable field, and any playbook line containing a digit is discarded. Where no data exist for a
182	supplier, all trades and weaknesses are removed, so that the agent asserts nothing that our
183	records do not support.
184	
185	Concurrently, code derives a supplier profile from fixed textual patterns (liquidity need, spare or full capacity, frozen prices, hedged material), takes our should-cost as the estimate of the supplier's full cost, and infers its cost of capital from ownership type, liquidity need and cash discount. It also constructs a claim bank of statements, each
186	citing a record identifier and the corresponding figure. Every figure the agent cites is drawn from this bank.
187	
188	\section{Offer policy}
189	
190	\paragraph{Terms} Every admissible combination of non-price terms is evaluated at the supplier's
191	own valuation, and the combination yielding the lowest \C{} is retained. A term that is
192	inexpensive for the buyer yet valuable to the supplier, such as accelerated payment for a
193	supplier with a liquidity need, can thus be exchanged for a price reduction.
194	
195	\paragraph{Opening} The opening price is a fraction of the quotation set by the category goal, from 0.78 in leverage cases to 0.92 in stabilisation cases, and raised for suppliers with frozen prices, full capacity or near-cost pricing. The trades proposed by
196	the lead are issued as additional term sheets, each priced so that its cost to the buyer equals
197	that of the principal sheet. The supplier's choice among them consequently reveals its
198	preferences at no cost to the buyer.
199	
200	\paragraph{Subsequent rounds} In each round the agent closes 30\,\% of the gap to the supplier's
201	counter-offer. Following a large initial concession it holds its position, as such a concession
202	indicates a low reservation price. A patience budget limits the gaps presented under relationship-oriented goals and to suppliers priced near cost or operating at full capacity. A live analyst reads each reply in at most six seconds and returns a mood, one of three tactics and the anticipated argument it has
203	recognised: \emph{hold} reduces the step to 70\,\% of its usual size, whereas \emph{argue} adds
204	one further claim. Three claims accompany the first message and one each subsequent message.
205	
206	\paragraph{Bounds} No term sheet exceeds the price at which \C{} equals the cost of the buyer's
207	alternative. Under relationship-oriented goals, where a full-cost estimate exists, the price is not reduced beyond the point at which the relationship charge causes \C{} to increase.
208	
209	\paragraph{Correspondence} The writer model responds to the supplier's most recent message in a
210	single paragraph free of digits and identifiers. Code appends the figures, claims and term
211	sheets, and substitutes a template whenever the paragraph is late or rejected.
212	
213	\section{Termination}
214	
215	The agent concludes a case upon the supplier's final offer, upon its agreement, at the round
216	limit, or 90 seconds before the time limit of a wave. Only a standing term sheet is eligible for
217	acceptance. A sheet qualifies if it states a price, satisfies every mandatory requirement and
218	costs less than the buyer's alternative. Among several bidders, the qualifying sheet of lowest
219	cost is accepted. If no sheet qualifies, the agent withdraws. Where a supplier's final offer would be refused, the agent first submits one sheet at its price ceiling.
220	
221	\section{Empirical observations on supplier behaviour}
222	
223	The policy encodes observations drawn from our own sandbox and practice sessions. Most are
224	supported by one to three sessions per supplier and should be read accordingly.
225	\begin{itemize}
226	\item Suppliers conceded in response to a move on our part and agreed once our sheet reached the
227	  counter-offer they would otherwise make; an unchanged sheet drew no concession in the sandbox.
228	  Our sheet is therefore kept below their counter-offer until the close.
229	\item Verified claims that a supplier took into account lowered its counter-offers, mostly in the
230	  first round: one sandbox supplier countered at 51.30 without claims and at 50.00 with three.
231	  Claims a supplier judged irrelevant had no effect.
232	\item Final offers arrived at a cumulative gap of 36 to 42\,\% between our sheets and their
233	  counter-offers in four cases, and at 15 to 19\,\% for two less patient suppliers.
234	\item Bluffs and urgency left the price unchanged (three runs each), and all twelve suppliers
235	  asked declined to share a cost breakdown. The agent employs none of these.
236	\end{itemize}
237	After each practice case, code records the price trajectories and the score, and a debrief model
238	contributes one lesson, indexed by goal, case type, ownership type and observed traits. The lead
239	researcher is given the figures of the most similar earlier runs rather than the written lesson,
240	since a single run constitutes an observation and not a rule.
241	
242	\section{Results and limitations}
243	
244	One practice session per hosted case yielded ten agreements in ten cases and a mean score of
245	0.777. In that batch, 121 of 127 subagent invocations succeeded, and the six that timed out
246	reverted to the deterministic path. On the preceding evening the mean of our best scores per case was 0.776, third of 24 on the practice leaderboard.
247	
248	Two limitations should be noted. First, a single batch does not constitute a paired comparison,
249	and we are unable to demonstrate that the subagent team outperforms the price engine alone.
250	Second, the full-cost estimate depends on a should-cost figure with a stated error of 5 to
251	15\,\%; the agent mitigates this by bounding the estimate with the supplier's own offers.
